# alxbl/aur

Source-only PKGBUILD repository for binary tools without official or AUR packages. Paru consumes it as a custom `[alxbl]` repository (`Url = https://github.com/alxbl/aur`). Nothing is pre-built.

## Procedures
- New package: start from `templates/PKGBUILD-bin` and add `update/<pkgname>.sh` in the same change. `update/check-all.sh` fails for a package without an updater.
- After any PKGBUILD change, regenerate `.SRCINFO`: `makepkg --printsrcinfo > .SRCINFO`.
- Bump packages with `update/check-all.sh`; review the diff before committing. `-n` only reports.
- Run `update/test.sh` after changing `update/lib.sh` or `update/check-all.sh`.
- A package that ships a script has `<pkgname>/test.sh`; run it after changing the script. Pin the sha256 of local source files in the PKGBUILD; the test checks they are current.
- Build and test packages in a scratch copy, not in the worktree. To try paru against the repo, use a scratch `paru.conf` via `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and `XDG_CACHE_HOME`.

## Traps
- Never edit the user's real `paru.conf` without consent.
- Never run `paru -Sy` against the real pacman databases for testing; it risks a partial upgrade. Use `paru --dbpath <copy> -Sy --pkgbuilds`.
- Paru finds packages by scanning for files named `PKGBUILD`. Never give a template or fixture that name.
- Pin `sha256sums`; never `SKIP`. The template's all-zero checksum is a deliberate placeholder.
- Updaters only rewrite the first element of a checksum array, the upstream download. An update that needs more (new dependencies, changed sources) must be flagged as out of date, not guessed.
- ACT must run on the game's exact wine build and sync mode (fsync/esync), or a second wineserver starts and ACT cannot see the game. The `advanced-combat-tracker` launcher follows the running wineserver for this; keep it that way.
- Bash uses dynamic scoping: a variable in a test or updater with the same name as a `local` in `run_update` (`current`, `latest`, `sums`, `work`, `array`, `sum`) is shadowed.
